hi there steve kaufman here we're still  early in the new year  today i'm going to talk about writing  but before i do that i want to remind  you that it is early in the new year  i've done a few videos now  even in japanese encouraging people to  be enthusiastic and to make the  commitment and we talked about setting  goals for you know your new year's  resolutions and it reminds me of the  joke about the guy who said you know i  joined the gym a year ago and i'm still  not feeling any fitter so i decided to  go down to the gym to see what was going  on like in other words he joined the gym  paid his money and never did anything  a lot of people joined gyms at the  beginning of the year and never do  anything  now is the time of the year to make a  commitment to learning or to improving  in your language  everyone can do it with the right  attitude with the enthusiasm by the way  at the end of this video there will be a  link you can click through and get a 40  discount on an annual membership in link  annual membership is the way to go  commit for the year but don't do like  that guy and then never uh study commit  for the year and then work on it every  day now writing i talk a lot about  listening and reading and speaking  and i say that listening and reading is  what's going to build up your vocabulary  improve your familiarity with the  language get the brain used to the  language  speaking you have to speak a lot in  order to be able to speak well now what  about writing  now i have to admit that the only  language where i have to write or had to  write a lot was french in my own  personal situation  and all the languages the other  languages that i've learned including  japanese when i lived in japan  uh i didn't need to write  and  but writing is a very useful way to  improve your language skills  uh in my chinese when i was learning  chinese and i had to write the british  foreign service exam i had to be able to  write a diplomatic note in chinese so  that was a requirement of the exam but  in real life i've never had to write so  depending on what our needs are we may  not have a need to write i lived in  japan i can't write i can't  write by hand in japanese i can  communicate you know via email using uh  you know computer software and the same  in other languages however  writing is so key because when you are  writing  you have the time  to look up a point of grammar that  you're  concerned about which you can't do when  you're speaking when you're speaking it  just comes out and when you write you  can review what you have written and you  can find mistakes because we do make  mistakes and sometimes when we review  something we've written  we'll say well we're not sure about this  i'll look it up or we know that this is  wrong and we can correct it  all of those things are not available  when we speak so writing is a great way  to sort of improve your accuracy in the  language because as you correct those  mistakes in writing you start to ingrain  some better habits so  i i have to admit that i don't have the  patience to write i just like to get my  favorite podcast and listen to it and  maybe get on youtube and talk to someone  or  read something and but i'm not i have no  need however  you know where writing accuracy comes  into play  if you're if you're working and the  language of business is  the language that you're learning  then there is a greater requirement for  you to be accurate especially in letters  but even in emails if if you write poor  english or spanish or chinese it sits  there people look at it it creates a  poorer impression if you're speaking to  someone your back and forth thing you  you can tell especially if you're  looking at the person you can tell what  they mean they can tell what you mean  it's not so crucial when you write you  have to be more accurate and so  writing is more demanding  um  now how do you get to write better you  know  this is the big question so i say to  speak well you have to speak a lot  uh to increase your vocabulary you have  to read a lot to develop some momentum  for your speaking and for your reading  you have to listen a lot now to write  well you have to both read a lot and  write a lot  so reading a lot because when you read  you're going to  get the words and the phrasing that you  need  because you don't have it within you  and then when you write you have an  opportunity  to look at what you've written and  correct it  um but but writing is more than that  writing is as i said earlier it's a way  of improving all of your language skills  if you have the patience to do it  uh and of course there's several aspects  to writing if you are learning a  language which has a different writing  system you have to begin by getting used  to the letters or in the case of chinese  and japanese they're characters so there  i think for example our dictation  function at link is very useful i used  it for greek i use it for arabic i used  it but i gave up in hebrew but that's  where you can hear the text to speech of  a phrase  the phrase you saved and then you have  to type it out so that's one way of  getting used to  the writing system however to be able to  write well to use words accurately to  have natural phrasing with fewer  mistakes you have to read a lot  and sometimes you hear people talking  about business english or academic  english i don't really see  that such things exist  but  you know if you're speaking to someone  in business  you know if i'm doing business in japan  in lumber i have to know the terms  for lumber that's all the rest of it is  not business english we're communicating  it's our japanese rather it's just  japanese so in your normal communication  with people there is no such thing in my  opinion as business english or business  japanese but in the written form there  is because there's a way to structure  your written communication the same is  true with academic  english or japanese if you read a lot on  medicine or whatever your area of  specialization is you will acquire  those  technical terms and the rest of it is  basically the language however  it's it's in structuring that  communication that you need to develop a  certain skill set  when i was in france at sean's post  lance d to do politik political science  institute they had a very  definite set  format you had to use you had to have  the introduction and the first section  the second section and the conclusion  and in the first section it had to be  balanced two parts a and b and in the  second section which was often sort of a  refutation you know refuting the first  section or expanding on the first  section but also in equal parts and then  finally a conclusion so this was the  format that i had to use it doesn't  matter what format you use but when  you're communicating in writing you  should have some sense of how you want  to structure it so that what do you put  you have to basically let the person  know what you're going to talk about  early on because they don't want to read  through this long screen to find out at  the end that you want to have a meeting  with them you better put that up front  but then how do you structure that and  what are the reasons you give and all of  this you should have a plan for and i  think there it is useful to read  and hopefully in your target language  because these  conventions can be different in  different languages it's different in  french in english and japanese and so  forth read up on how business  communication academic communication is  structured so that you have that sort of  formula in mind and then it's easier to  write whatever you have to write a  report or a letter however the actual  language used consists of  language  plus whatever technical terms  are you know relevant to that particular  discipline  however if you're in that discipline  you're likely to know those technical  terms or if you don't know them you  better learn them but the bigger issue  is  getting the structure of your  communication correct and then of course  acquiring the words and phrases that  make written  the written language flow smoothly you  know i wish to point out or whatever it  might be and these phrases you have to  see them often if you're on link you  save the phrases you review the phrases  so you build up your written language  with these phrases  and finally i have found when i do write  in another language i consider it an  extension of my speaking so i try to  avoid speaking in a foreign language in  a very sort of slang casual  colorful way i try to speak  a neutral form of french spanish  japanese whatever it may be and then i  use that same  form of the language when i write so  that the actual the language  sort of uh itself is more or less the  same except in writing you're going to  be more specific you're going to be more  concise you're going to go back in and  correct things you're going to try to  conform to a certain structure so you  have to pay more attention and that is  in itself a very good discipline for all  of your language learning so  as we head into  2020  uh you know commit to your language  learning  maybe if you have the time and the  patience do more writing and of course  sign up for a year at link using our 40  discount  and again we'll see you  next week bye for now
 

